{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791546121,"nonce":"sqxmGwFA2+jjdu+whbI0Y62M9oc=","title":"The idle handback path only considers blocks freed by the current sweep. During a busy allocation burst, sweep deliberately skips madvise to avoid repeated REUSABLE/REUSE churn. Those previously freed resident blocks are then absent from `freed` in the later idle sweep, so they are never handed back. Idle eligibility also checks only allocation pressure; after an explicit major clears pressure, it can skip idle work despite many resident free blocks.","message":"Source: std/src/gc.rs, sweep handback condition `quiet \u0026\u0026 !freed.is_empty()` and idle_collector's `worth` predicate.\n\nObserved lead: Ashui TreeMotion reusable-target replay, settled with two explicit majors, shows a 128 MiB writable mapping with 52.4 MiB dirty/resident. Attribution to this precise path still needs the before/after runtime experiment; live malloc alone cannot explain that mapping. Other native allocations are tracked separately.\n\nPlan: track only previously used free blocks awaiting handback, remove candidates whenever small or large allocation reacquires them, and drain surplus once the process is idle. Virgin reserved blocks must never be counted as resident candidates, and active/TLAB blocks must never be returned. Preserve the existing busy-workload policy and balanced macOS REUSABLE/REUSE ledger. Test reclamation followed by reuse and compare correctness, footprint, and throughput.","files":null}]}